<div class="section" id="g_t_0418_0441_043f_043e_043b_044c_0437_043e_0432_0430_043d_0438_0435-_043a_043b_044e_0447_0435_0439-_0431_0435_0437_043e_043f_0430_0441_043d_043e_0441_0442_0438">
<div class="header">
<p>
Next: <a href="Dinamicheskaya-zadacha-DNS-mcron.html" accesskey="n" rel="next">Динамическая задача DNS mcron</a>, Previous: <a href="API-obraza-Guix-sistemy.html" accesskey="p" rel="prev">API образа Guix системы</a>, Up: <a href="Nastroika-sistemy.html" accesskey="u" rel="up">Настройка системы</a> &nbsp; [<a href="index.html#SEC_Contents" title="Table of contents" rel="contents">Contents</a>][<a href="Terminy-i-ukazateli.html" title="Index" rel="index">Index</a>]</p>
</div>
<hr />
<span id="Ispolxzovanie-kliuchei-bezopasnosti"></span><h3 class="section">3.4 Использование ключей безопасности</h3>
<span id="index-2FA_002c-two_002dfactor-authentication"></span>
<span id="index-U2F_002c-Universal-2nd-Factor"></span>
<span id="index-security-key_002c-configuration"></span>

<p>The use of security keys can improve your security by providing a second
authentication source that cannot be easily stolen or copied, at least for a
remote adversary (something that you have), to the main secret (a passphrase
&ndash; something that you know), reducing the risk of impersonation.
</p>
<p>The example configuration detailed below showcases what minimal
configuration needs to be made on your Guix System to allow the use of a
Yubico security key.  It is hoped the configuration can be useful for other
security keys as well, with minor adjustments.
</p>
<ul class="section-toc">
<li><a href="#Configuration-for-use-as-a-two_002dfactor-authenticator-_00282FA_0029" accesskey="1">Configuration for use as a two-factor authenticator (2FA)</a></li>
<li><a href="#Disabling-OTP-code-generation-for-a-Yubikey" accesskey="2">Disabling OTP code generation for a Yubikey</a></li>
<li><a href="#Requiring-a-Yubikey-to-open-a-KeePassXC-database" accesskey="3">Requiring a Yubikey to open a KeePassXC database</a></li>
</ul>
<div class="subsection" id="Configuration-for-use-as-a-two_002dfactor-authenticator-_00282FA_0029">
<h4 class="subsection">3.4.1 Configuration for use as a two-factor authenticator (2FA)</h4>

<p>To be usable, the udev rules of the system should be extended with
key-specific rules.  The following shows how to extend your udev rules with
the <samp>lib/udev/rules.d/70-u2f.rules</samp> udev rule file provided by the
<code>libfido2</code> package from the <code>(gnu packages security-token)</code> module
and add your user to the &lsquo;<samp>"plugdev"</samp>&rsquo; group it uses:
</p>
<div class="example lisp">
<pre class="lisp"><span class="syntax-paren0">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-symbol">use-package-modules</span> <a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a> <span class="syntax-symbol">security-token</span> <a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a></span>)</span>
<a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a>
<span class="syntax-paren0">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><a class="syntax-symbol" href="/manual/devel/ru/html_node/Interfeis-operating_002dsystem.html#index-operating_002dsystem-1">operating-system</a>
 <a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a>
 <span class="syntax-paren1">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-symbol">users</span> <span class="syntax-paren2">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/List-Constructors.html#index-cons_002a">cons*</a> <span class="syntax-paren3">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><a class="syntax-symbol" href="/manual/devel/ru/html_node/Uchiotnye-zapisi-polxzovatelei.html#index-user_002daccount">user-account</a>
               <span class="syntax-paren4">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-symbol">name</span> <span class="syntax-string">"your-user"</span></span>)</span>
               <span class="syntax-paren4">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-symbol">group</span> <span class="syntax-string">"users"</span></span>)</span>
               <span class="syntax-paren4">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-symbol">supplementary-groups</span>
		<span class="syntax-symbol">'</span><span class="syntax-paren5">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-string">"wheel"</span> <span class="syntax-string">"netdev"</span> <span class="syntax-string">"audio"</span> <span class="syntax-string">"video"</span>
                  <span class="syntax-string">"plugdev"</span></span>)</span></span>)</span>           <span class="syntax-comment">;&lt;- added system group
</span>               <span class="syntax-paren4">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-symbol">home-directory</span> <span class="syntax-string">"/home/your-user"</span></span>)</span></span>)</span>
              <a class="syntax-symbol" href="/manual/devel/ru/html_node/Uchiotnye-zapisi-polxzovatelei.html#index-_0025base_002duser_002daccounts">%base-user-accounts</a></span>)</span></span>)</span>
 <a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a>
 <span class="syntax-paren1">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-symbol">services</span>
  <span class="syntax-paren2">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/List-Constructors.html#index-cons_002a">cons*</a>
   <a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a>
   <span class="syntax-paren3">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><a class="syntax-symbol" href="/manual/devel/ru/html_node/Bazovye-sluzhby.html#index-udev_002drules_002dservice">udev-rules-service</a> <span class="syntax-symbol">'fido2</span> <span class="syntax-symbol">libfido2</span> <span class="syntax-keyword">#:groups</span> <span class="syntax-symbol">'</span><span class="syntax-paren4">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-string">"plugdev"</span></span>)</span></span>)</span></span>)</span></span>)</span></span>)</span>
</pre></div>

<p>After re-configuring your system and re-logging in your graphical session so
that the new group is in effect for your user, you can verify that your key
is usable by launching:
</p>
<div class="example">
<pre class="example">guix shell ungoogled-chromium -- chromium chrome://settings/securityKeys
</pre></div>

<p>and validating that the security key can be reset via the &ldquo;Reset your
security key&rdquo; menu.  If it works, congratulations, your security key is
ready to be used with applications supporting two-factor authentication
(2FA).
</p>
</div>
<div class="subsection" id="Disabling-OTP-code-generation-for-a-Yubikey">
<h4 class="subsection">3.4.2 Disabling OTP code generation for a Yubikey</h4>
<span id="index-disabling-yubikey-OTP"></span>
<p>If you use a Yubikey security key and are irritated by the spurious OTP
codes it generates when inadvertently touching the key (e.g. causing you to
become a spammer in the &lsquo;<samp>#guix</samp>&rsquo; channel when discussing from your
favorite IRC client!), you can disable it via the following <code>ykman</code>
command:
</p>
<div class="example">
<pre class="example">guix shell python-yubikey-manager -- ykman config usb --force --disable OTP
</pre></div>

<p>Alternatively, you could use the <code>ykman-gui</code> command provided by the
<code>yubikey-manager-qt</code> package and either wholly disable the &lsquo;<samp>OTP</samp>&rsquo;
application for the USB interface or, from the &lsquo;<samp>Applications -&gt; OTP</samp>&rsquo;
view, delete the slot 1 configuration, which comes pre-configured with the
Yubico OTP application.
</p>
</div>
<div class="subsection" id="Requiring-a-Yubikey-to-open-a-KeePassXC-database">
<h4 class="subsection">3.4.3 Requiring a Yubikey to open a KeePassXC database</h4>
<span id="index-yubikey_002c-keepassxc-integration"></span>
<p>The KeePassXC password manager application has support for Yubikeys, but it
requires installing a udev rules for your Guix System and some configuration
of the Yubico OTP application on the key.
</p>
<p>The necessary udev rules file comes from the <code>yubikey-personalization</code>
package, and can be installed like:
</p>
<div class="example lisp">
<pre class="lisp"><span class="syntax-paren0">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-symbol">use-package-modules</span> <a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a> <span class="syntax-symbol">security-token</span> <a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a></span>)</span>
<a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a>
<span class="syntax-paren0">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><a class="syntax-symbol" href="/manual/devel/ru/html_node/Interfeis-operating_002dsystem.html#index-operating_002dsystem-1">operating-system</a>
 <a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a>
 <span class="syntax-paren1">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><span class="syntax-symbol">services</span>
  <span class="syntax-paren2">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/List-Constructors.html#index-cons_002a">cons*</a>
   <a class="syntax-symbol" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/Syntax-Rules.html#index-_002e_002e_002e">...</a>
   <span class="syntax-paren3">(<span class="syntax-symbol"><a class="syntax-symbol" href="/manual/devel/ru/html_node/Bazovye-sluzhby.html#index-udev_002drules_002dservice">udev-rules-service</a> <span class="syntax-symbol">'yubikey</span> <span class="syntax-symbol">yubikey-personalization</span></span>)</span></span>)</span></span>)</span></span>)</span>
</pre></div>

<p>After reconfiguring your system (and reconnecting your Yubikey), you&rsquo;ll then
want to configure the OTP challenge/response application of your Yubikey on
its slot 2, which is what KeePassXC uses.  It&rsquo;s easy to do so via the
Yubikey Manager graphical configuration tool, which can be invoked with:
</p>
<div class="example">
<pre class="example">guix shell yubikey-manager-qt -- ykman-gui
</pre></div>

<p>First, ensure &lsquo;<samp>OTP</samp>&rsquo; is enabled under the &lsquo;<samp>Interfaces</samp>&rsquo; tab, then
navigate to &lsquo;<samp>Applications -&gt; OTP</samp>&rsquo;, and click the &lsquo;<samp>Configure</samp>&rsquo;
button under the &lsquo;<samp>Long Touch (Slot 2)</samp>&rsquo; section.  Select
&lsquo;<samp>Challenge-response</samp>&rsquo;, input or generate a secret key, and click the
&lsquo;<samp>Finish</samp>&rsquo; button.  If you have a second Yubikey you&rsquo;d like to use as a
backup, you should configure it the same way, using the <em>same</em> secret
key.
</p>
<p>Your Yubikey should now be detected by KeePassXC.  It can be added to a
database by navigating to KeePassXC&rsquo;s &lsquo;<samp>Database -&gt; Database
Security...</samp>&rsquo; menu, then clicking the &lsquo;<samp>Add additional protection...</samp>&rsquo;
button, then &lsquo;<samp>Add Challenge-Response</samp>&rsquo;, selecting the security key from
the drop-down menu and clicking the &lsquo;<samp>OK</samp>&rsquo; button to complete the setup.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<hr />
